From 26374ebe197dff481944b135c80cc1a49a16d215 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 13:19:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: let a live stream start and stay at its live
 edge

The clock for a stream is started from the first frame that decodes. For a
real time stream that turns however long the stream players took to get
going into a presentation latency lasting the whole session: the source
keeps producing in real time, so the player stays exactly as far behind as
it was when it started, and nothing brings it back.

Two things feed that delay and they trade off against each other, so
neither is small. Waiting for the first picture to decode took between
0.45s and 1.3s in testing, varying with how fast frames arrive; and where
an audio stream is present the clock is additionally started cachetotal +
400ms behind, which is 0.74s with an audio sink reporting 800ms. Screen
mirroring measured 1.3-1.6s of latency inside the player as a result.

Add INPUTSTREAM_LOW_LATENCY_LIVE so a source can say it would rather be
close to live than keep that buffer. When set, sync starts the clock from
the newest timestamp demuxed rather than the first one decoded, and the
frames queued ahead of it are dropped as late.

Off unless asked for: the delay is a jitter buffer, and for broadcast
sources it is doing useful work. It is worth trading only where being close
to the source is the point, as it is for a mirrored screen.

Starting there is not enough on its own, because three things then give the
latency back over the life of a session.

A stall is taken to mean the source is in trouble, and the player buffers
until it refills. For a source that asked to stay at the live edge that is
the wrong trade twice over: the gap is usually just the source having
nothing new to send -- a mirrored screen that no one is touching sends
nothing at all -- and the pause is time it can never win back, so every one
of them is added to the delay for the rest of the session. Two seconds of
buffering measured on a mirroring session became two seconds of permanent
latency. Treat it the way a menu or a still image is already treated: a
stall is not news, so leave the clock running and carry on when there is
something to show.

A live stream whose audio queue runs low has the clock slowed by 5% to give
it a chance to refill, and that is undone when the queue recovers past a
threshold. Both halves sit behind a test for the audio stream existing, so
a stream that disappears mid-playback -- an inputstream withdrawing a track
that has gone silent, for one -- leaves the clock slowed with nothing able
to restore it. It then falls 50ms further behind the source every second
for the rest of the session, which for a live source it can never make
back. Restore normal speed when the branch that would otherwise undo it no
longer runs. The same reasoning covers passthrough, which is excluded just
above because its queue level never rises far enough to trigger the
restore.

And the margin left in hand at the start was a fixed 100ms, which is a
guess: how much room a source needs depends on how evenly it arrives, and
there is no single answer. A screen being mirrored delivers video steadily
but sound only while there is a sound to send, so it turns up in bursts of
a couple of hundred milliseconds -- more than the margin, so the audio
engine resamples it back into place every few seconds and every correction
is audible. Take the value from the stream instead, as a listitem property,
defaulting to the 100ms used until now. A property rather than a new call:
the function table is allocated by Kodi, so adding to it breaks any add-on
built against a newer header than the running build, and a name costs
nothing.

Backported to Kodi 21.3-Omega (a3a448d) for the OSMC vero3 build from
popcornmix/xbmc@e18e062e5 (branch airplay). Omega's CDVDInputStream has no
IsStreaming(), so the new accessors follow IsRealtime(), and the header
includes <chrono> itself. The inputstream API version stays at 3.3.0: the
flag and the stream property only add to it. The passthrough remark above
does not apply here: Omega has no passthrough exclusion in that block, so
the restore only covers a real time stream whose audio stream has gone
away.
